 entirely of \u03b1-ketoacids (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-020-00560-7)\n  20. Formation of urea and guanidine by irradiation of ammonium cyanide (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01660246)\n  21. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a (https://europepmc.org/api/getPdf?pmcid=PMC4568310)\n  22. Cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanosulfidic_prebiotic_synthesis)\n  23. Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.07725)\n  24. Guanidine synthesis by guanylation (https://www.organic-chemistry.org/synthesis/C1N/guanidines.shtm)\n  25. Extraterrestrial amino acids and L\u2010enantiomeric excesses in the \\<fc\\>CM\\</fc\\>2 carbonaceous chondrites Aguas Zarcas and Murchison - Glavin - 2021 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/maps.13451)\n  26. The stereochemistry of amino acids in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0301926800001236)\n  27. Purines and triazines in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0016703775901015)\n  28. Extraterrestrial nucleobases in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012821X08001866?via=ihub%3D)\n  29. (PDF) The Hydrolysis of Arginine (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/9309353_The_Hydrolysis_of_Arginine)\n  30. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein ... (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=167961801406af19852f76f62e796fa905c99643&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\n  31. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein amino acids - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JMolE..17..273W)\n  32. Consensus temporal order of amino acids and evolution of the triplet code (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12166018_Consensus_temporal_order_of_amino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"No defended prebiotic experiment reaches free arginine, so the network prioritizes the two least-bad evidence-weighted families rather than disputed direct-arginine claims. The main family is Patel-style surface cyanosulfidic chemistry, which builds the C5 guanidino skeleton to the experimentally supported arginine alpha-aminonitrile through acrylonitrile, beta-aminopropionitrile, and Patel intermediates 35 and 37; hydrolysis to arginine is included but explicitly marked speculative. A second, weaker family treats ornithine as a late abiotic intermediate from HCN-rich chemistry and converts it to arginine by cyanamide guanidinylation, again flagged as unverified for the free amino acid. The citrulline/urea-cycle analogue and hydrothermal keto-acid routes are omitted as productive branches because the supplied literature offers only untested or purely thermodynamic support. The 10 pathways therefore combine four de novo cyanosulfidic routes, two ornithine endgames, and four shorter modular entry routes from advanced intermediates.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-guanidinopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"guanidinylated beta-aminopropionitrile (Patel intermediate 35)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-guanidinopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"guanidinylated beta-aminopropionaldehyde (Patel intermediate 37)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H13N5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine alpha-aminonitrile (Patel intermediate 46)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical cyanamide generation from HCN and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated HCN/NH3-rich surface waters; ammonium cyanide forms in situ; days to weeks\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical conversion of ammonium cyanide derived from HCN and NH3 into cyanamide, with urea and guanidine as likely coproduct classes.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lohrmann (1972) showed that irradiation of aqueous ammonium cyanide gives cyanamide together with urea and guanidine. This justifies treating cyanamide as an endogenous surface feedstock, although the chemistry is not selective and would operate as a mixed product pool rather than as a clean preparative step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu(I)-mediated coupling of HCN and acetylene to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate-buffered photoredox\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-exposed cyanosulfidic streams or pools; localized copper-bearing settings required\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu(I)-mediated hydrocyanation or cross-coupling of acetylene with HCN gives acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) experimentally placed acrylonitrile at the entry to the arginine branch using Cu(I) in a phosphate-buffered UV cyanosulfidic network. This is the best-supported C3 skeleton-forming step specific to the arginine-precursor branch in the supplied literature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrylonitrile ammonolysis to beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich near-neutral aqueous solution in phosphate-buffered surface settings\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Conjugate addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile forms the beta-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) identify beta-aminopropionitrile 34 from acrylonitrile plus ammonia as the common entry point for later arginine chemistry. This Michael-type addition is chemically straightforward and among the better grounded steps in the network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"HS-/UV reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu photoredox with phosphate buffering\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation; HS-/H2S acts as stoichiometric photoreductant\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfur-assisted photoreduction converts the nitrile to an aldehyde via reductive sulfur chemistry and hydrolytic workup.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report H2S-driven photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile 34 to beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36, quoted in the review at 45% for this step. Because the chemistry depends on sulfidic photoredox, it inherits the locality and concentration constraints discussed by Ranjan et al. (2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanamide-bearing pool; near-neutral to mildly basic pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Addition of the terminal amine to cyanamide followed by proton transfers gives a guanidino-substituted nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) directly converted 34 with cyanamide to the guanidinylated nitrile 35, but explicitly described the reaction as relatively inefficient. It is nevertheless the main experimentally anchored route that installs the guanidino functionality before later chain extension.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide addition to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanamide present together with the aldehyde intermediate in a surface flow system\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reaction of the terminal amine with cyanamide gives a guanidinylated aminoaldehyde adduct, represented here as 3-guanidinopropanal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) observed that residual cyanamide reacts very efficiently with beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36 to give adduct 37 and suggested this ordering helps feed the arginine-precursor branch. This aldehyde-first guanidinylation step is therefore better supported than free-ornithine guanidination, although the exact integrated flow sequence remains conditional in the paper.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative reduction of guanidinylated beta-aminopropionitrile to the aldehyde analogue\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu photoredox with phosphate buffering\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation; speculative ordering variant of the cyanosulfidic sequence\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Speculative sulfur-assisted photoreduction of the guanidinylated nitrile to the corresponding aldehyde, analogous to 34 to 36.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No reaction in the supplied literature explicitly reports 35 converting to the aldehyde analogue of 37. This step is therefore speculative, but Patel et al. (2015) show the closely related 34 to 36 nitrile-to-aldehyde reduction and thus justify including a guanidinylate-first, reduce-later ordering as a chemically reasonable alternative branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Late cyanosulfidic homologation to arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu photoredox with phosphate buffering\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-driven sequential flow chemistry through HCN homologation, thioamide and cyanohydrin stages, then Strecker addition\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN homologation and HS-/UV reduction convert the guanidinylated C3 aldehyde into a longer aldehyde-equivalent; subsequent Strecker chemistry with HCN and NH3 installs the alpha-aminonitrile to give arginine alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) tabulate the late sequence culminating in 46, the alpha-aminonitrile precursor of arginine, and frame integrated production conditionally rather than as a clean one-pot synthesis. This reaction compresses those documented late homologation and Strecker operations into a single network edge, and it therefore remains literature-guided but partly inferred; the required sulfidic setting is also environmentally local rather than globally guaranteed (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mild hydrolytic conditions in pools or geothermal cycling; strong acid or base avoided because guanidino degradation is a concern\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the alpha-aminonitrile into the alpha-amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the critical missing endgame: Patel et al. (2015) stop at the arginine alpha-aminonitrile and do not report free arginine. The review explicitly notes that no unambiguous prebiotic experiment has completed this hydrolysis, and older HCN-polymer hydrolysis assignments are confounded by non-reproducibility and side-product formation (Ferris et al., 1973; Ferris et al., 1974). This conversion is therefore necessary but explicitly speculative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Net warm HCN/NH3 route to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated HCN/NH3 or NH4CN-rich warm pools; represented as a net route rather than a single elementary step\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN oligomerization or polymerization followed by aqueous breakdown releases trace diamino-acid products, represented here as a net formation of ornithine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) summarize older hot HCN and NH4CN experiments in which ornithine was reported, and modern reanalysis of Miller samples independently confirms that ornithine can be formed abiotically in reduced-gas chemistry (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011). As a net HCN/NH3 route this remains low-confidence and probably low-yield, but it is more defensible than claiming direct arginine from HCN polymers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative cyanamide guanidinylation of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces; prolonged residence or wet-dry concentration; alpha versus delta selectivity unresolved\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Terminal amine guanidinylation of ornithine by cyanamide gives arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Longo et al. (2020) demonstrated aqueous guanidination of ornithine side chains in a synthetic protein using a laboratory guanidinylating reagent and argued that cyanamide could serve as a primordial surrogate. However, no published prebiotic study has shown selective free ornithine plus cyanamide conversion to arginine in water, and Thoma and Powner (2023) highlight competing ornithine cyclization in related prebiotic chemistry. This route is chemically attractive as a late endgame but remains unverified for the free amino acid.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"