mino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.49\n- Feedback: The network topology is acceptable: 13 molecule nodes and 11 reaction nodes form a connected graph with consistent IDs. Literature alignment is better than the chemistry implementation: the strategy appropriately centers Patel-style cyanosulfidic chemistry, acknowledges that only the arginine alpha-aminonitrile is experimentally supported, treats ornithine guanidinylation as speculative, and does not rely on disputed direct-arginine claims. The limiting problem is validity and stoichiometry. RDKit finds 6 of 11 reactions unbalanced: rxn_001 likely needs H2 as coproduct; rxn_004 and rxn_007 cannot be represented as simple H2S-only reductions and likely omit H2O, NH3 and sulfur byproducts; rxn_008 is missing at least one carbon equivalent and compresses the 37 -> 45 -> 46 sequence into an unsupported one-step net; rxn_009 should include 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as product; rxn_010 is grossly under-specified and not a credible single-step ornithine synthesis. rxn_011 is balanced but still unverified for free ornithine with cyanamide, and delta-selectivity is unresolved. Surface conditions are the best fit for the Patel-like chemistry, but the final Biochemical steps are not mapped onto defended prebiotic environments. Best branch is P2, but even that route only plausibly reaches the arginine alpha-aminonitrile logic, not defended free arginine. Not approved as submitted.\n- Issues: ['Best endogenous de novo branch conceptually because it follows the literature-supported Patel aldehyde-first logic from acrylonitrile to intermediate 37 and then toward the arginine alpha-aminonitrile. The main problems are stoichiometric: rxn_001 is unbalanced and likely needs H2 as a coproduct, rxn_004 is not a valid H2S-only one-step conversion and likely omits H2O, NH3 and sulfur byproducts, rxn_008 collapses the documented multistep 37 -> 45 -> 46 chemistry into an unbalanced net that is short one carbon, and rxn_009 should include 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as product. Literature alignment is good, but free arginine is not actually defended as formed here, and the final Biochemical step is not placed in a specific prebiotic setting.', 'This is the strongest pathway in the network and the closest match to the Patel literature because it uses the experimentally favored aldehyde-first order. However, rxn_004, rxn_008 and rxn_009 are still unbalanced; rxn_008 in particular compresses several Patel steps and is short one carbon, while rxn_009 needs 2 H2O in and NH3 out. If cyanamide is intended to be environmental here, mol_004 should be declared as an explicit starting input or justified transfer. Surface chemistry is plausible up to the aminonitrile logic, but the final Biochemical hydrolysis is not a defended prebiotic environment.', 'The early steps to guanidinylated nitrile 35 are literature-supported, but Patel actually favors aldehyde-first capture because the 36 plus cyanamide reaction is efficient. This nitrile-first branch depends on rxn_007, an explicitly speculative and unbalanced 35-to-37 type reduction that likely omits H2O, NH3 and sulfur byproducts, then inherits the unbalanced compressed rxn_008 and the unbalanced hydrolysis rxn_009, which should include 2 H2O in and NH3 out. rxn_001 also needs H2 as coproduct if cyanamide is generated in-network.', 'Slightly cleaner than P3 only because it omits in-network cyanamide formation, but it remains substantially weaker than the aldehyde-first branch. rxn_007 is unbalanced and unsupported by the cited Patel chemistry, rxn_008 is an unbalanced one-step compression of a longer sequence and is short one carbon, and rxn_009 needs 2 H2O in and NH3 out. As written, this route is a speculative extrapolation rather than an experimentally grounded arginine pathway, and environmental cyanamide should be made explicit if that assumption is intended.', 'Strategically honest about the ornithine endgame being contested, but chemically weak as encoded. rxn_010 is grossly unbalanced and does not represent any single literature route from HCN plus NH3 to ornithine; ornithine reports come from HCN-polymer hydrolysates or spark residues, not direct one-step conversion. rxn_011 is balanced and chemically conceivable, but free ornithine guanidinylation by cyanamide in water remains unverified, alpha versus delta selectivity is unresolved, and the Biochemical environment label is too vague to count as a defended prebiotic setting. rxn_001 also needs H2 as coproduct.', 'This is the cleaner ornithine endgame because it avoids in-network cyanamide formation, but the core weakness remains. rxn_010 is still a severely unbalanced and under-specified stand-in for trace ornithine reports from very different chemistries, while rxn_011, although balanced, is not experimentally demonstrated for free ornithine under prebiotic aqueous conditions and has unresolved site selectivity. If cyanamide is environmental, that input should be explicit rather than implied.', 'As a module, this tracks the strongest Patel ordering from acrylonitrile through the aminoaldehyde and cyanamide adduct. As a complete synthesis it is incomplete because acrylonitrile is taken as given, and rxn_004, rxn_008 and rxn_009 still suffer the same balance and endgame problems as P2: rxn_004 is not a valid H2S-only net, rxn_008 is an unbalanced compression of the late homologation sequence, and rxn_009 should include 2 H2O in and NH3 out.', \"This module is weaker than P7 because it hinges on rxn_007, an unbalanced and explicitly speculative reduction from guanidinylated nitrile toward the aldehyde manifold that is not supported by Patel's reported sequence. Even before the final hydrolysis, rxn_008 remains an unbalanced one-step collapse of a longer late-stage homologation. The route is therefore only a tentative hypothesis around 35, not a validated module to arginine.\", 'Useful late-stage test module because the aminoaldehyde plus cyanamide step to intermediate 37 is experimentally strong. But the network still does not define a balanced route from 37 to the arginine aminonitrile: rxn_008 is unbalanced and missing at least one carbon equivalent, and rxn_009 remains an unbalanced and unverified hydrolysis to free arginine that should include 2 H2O in and NH3 out. So this is a plausible precursor module, not a demonstrated arginine module.', 'This late-stage guanidinylated nitrile module is weak because it depends on rxn_007, an unbalanced and explicitly speculative 35-to-37 type reduction that is not supported by the cited Patel work. Even if that step were repaired, rxn_008 is still missing carbon and compresses a longer sequence, while rxn_009 is an unbalanced and unverified hydrolysis to free arginine. The module is therefore not chemically robust as written.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network prioritizes surface cyanosulfidic chemistry because it is the only experimentally developed arginine-specific branch in the supplied literature: HCN plus acetylene gives acrylonitrile, which gives beta-aminopropionitrile, then a guanidino aldehyde manifold, then the arginine alpha-aminonitrile. The literature boundary is respected by marking the final hydrolysis to free arginine as speculative, since Patel et al. (2015) stop at the aminonitrile. In parallel, the network includes a late-stage ornithine-to-arginine guanidinylation route with cyanamide, also flagged as plausible but unverified for free ornithine. Ten pathways are listed as two full routes plus shorter valid entry routes from accumulated intermediates; disputed direct arginine claims from HCN polymer hydrolysates are not used as positive evidence, and hydrothermal routes are omitted because the supplied literature gives no arginine-specific kinetic pathway there.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h(H2,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c4-2-1-3-5/h1-2,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/S\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-(carbamimidamido)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"guanidinylated beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-(carbamimidamido)propanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"guanidinylated beta-aminopropionaldehyde adduct\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"N-(4-oxobutyl)guanidine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formal C5 guanidino aldehyde Strecker precursor\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H13N5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu(I)-catalyzed coupling of hydrogen cyanide with acetylene\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated near-neutral surface stream or pool; locally cyanide-rich cyanosulfidic setting\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-mediated C-C bond formation between HCN-derived cyanide and acetylene generates the activated nitrile alkene acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly use Cu(I)-catalyzed coupling of HCN with acetylene to make acrylonitrile 33 as the entry point to the arginine branch. A surficial UV-fed setting is preferred because the same chemistry is framed as local surface flow chemistry rather than hydrothermal vent chemistry; copper availability is a limitation, so this is best treated as a niche but experimentally grounded route (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly basic aqueous pool or stream; ammonia-rich\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia performs conjugate addition to the activated alkene of acrylonitrile, giving beta-aminopropionitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report conjugate addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile to give beta-aminopropionitrile 34, the common branch point toward arginine-related chemistry. This is a straightforward Michael-type addition and is one of the better-supported steps in the branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldehyde-first formation of the guanidino C3 aldehyde adduct\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation with HS-/H2S as stoichiometric photoreductant; sequential photoreductive nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion followed by cyanamide capture\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net two-step sequence: photoreductive hydrolysis of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde, then reaction of the aldehyde-bearing amine with cyanamide to form the guanidino aldehyde adduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reaction is written as a balanced net summary of the experimentally supported aldehyde-first sequence in Patel et al. (2015): 34->36 by H2S/HS--driven photoreduction, then efficient capture of 36 by cyanamide to give 37. Patel et al. report 34->36 at 45% and the combined 34->37 sequence at 77% over two steps. This is the literature-preferred ordering because the aldehyde reacts with cyanamide efficiently, whereas direct guanidinylation of 34 is described as relatively inefficient (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile with cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral aqueous pool; cyanamide present with ammonia-derived beta-aminopropionitrile\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The primary amine of beta-aminopropionitrile adds to cyanamide and rearranges/tautomerizes to the guanidino nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) directly convert 34 to the guanidinylated nitrile 35 with cyanamide, but explicitly note that this guanidinylation is relatively inefficient and leaves residual starting material and cyanamide. It is included as an experimentally observed but weaker alternative branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreductive hydrolysis of guanidino nitrile to the guanidino C3 aldehyde adduct\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation in sulfide-rich surface water; speculative nitrile-first continuation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net reductive hydrolysis of the terminal nitrile to the corresponding aldehyde under HS-/H2S photoredox conditions, releasing ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. No experiment in the supplied literature directly reports 35->37, but the chemistry is a close analogue of Patel's demonstrated photoreductive nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion 34->36. The balanced net written here captures the same nitrile-to-aldehyde logic after prior guanidinylation. This branch is therefore chemically plausible but untested, and may compete poorly with side reactions or decomposition (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Net one-carbon homologation to the formal C5 guanidino aldehyde precursor\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfide-rich pool; formal net representation of the late homologation manifold\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN adds one carbon to the guanidino aldehyde manifold, and sulfide-driven photoredox chemistry effects the reductive homologation needed to reach a C5 guanidino aldehyde-equivalent Strecker precursor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) place 37 on a late reductive homologation manifold proceeding through cyanohydrin/thioamide intermediates toward the arginine aminonitrile branch. The free aldehyde N-(4-oxobutyl)guanidine is not isolated there; in this network it is used as a formal node that summarizes the C4->C5 homologation state feeding the final Strecker step. This is therefore a literature-guided net step rather than a directly isolated one, but it follows the reported homologation logic and matches the independent use of the same aldehyde concept as the putative Strecker precursor in computational work (Patel et al., 2015; Cobb et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker conversion to arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN/NH3-rich pool; near-neutral to mildly basic aqueous conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The C5 guanidino aldehyde undergoes Strecker chemistry with ammonia and cyanide to give the corresponding alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) tabulate the late 45->46 conversion to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile at 90%. Because their exact isolated precursor is a late homologation intermediate rather than the free aldehyde formalized here, this node is represented as the corresponding Strecker event from the C5 guanidino aldehyde precursor. The chemistry is standard Strecker logic and is the strongest experimentally supported route currently available to an arginine-specific direct precursor (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Prolonged mild hydrolysis in surface pools or geothermal cycling; strong acid/base avoided\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha-aminonitrile hydrates and hydrolyzes to the amino acid carboxylate level, releasing ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and explicitly the key unverified endgame. The supplied literature does not report hydrolysis of the arginine alpha-aminonitrile to free arginine under defended prebiotic conditions; Patel et al. (2015) stop at the aminonitrile, and the review emphasizes that no complete prebiotic synthesis of free arginine has been demonstrated. Mild hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles is chemically reasonable, but harsh acid/base treatment risks guanidino-group degradation to citrulline or ornithine, so this step is included as a necessary but presently unsupported closure (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidinylation of ornithine with cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated evaporitic or geothermal pools; cyanamide-rich, slow aqueous guanidinylation expected\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amino group of ornithine adds to cyanamide and rearranges/tautomerizes to the guanidino side chain of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative but chemically attractive if ornithine is available. Ornithine itself is reported from spark-discharge and hot HCN/NH4CN chemistries, whereas arginine is not (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). Longo et al. (2020) showed aqueous ornithine-side-chain guanidination in a synthetic protein and argued that cyanamide could serve prebiotically; cyanamide availability is also supported by NH4CN photochemistry (Lohrmann, 1972). Patel et al. (2015) further show that cyanamide can guanidinylate aliphatic amines, albeit inefficiently. Free-ornithine delta-selective guanidinylation by cyanamide in water has not been directly demonstrated, so this route remains plausible but unverified (Longo et al., 2020; Patel et al., 2015; Thoma, 2023).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Full cyanosulfidic aldehyde-first route\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete surface route from acetylene, HCN, NH3, cyanamide, H2S, and water through the experimentally favored aldehyde-first sequence to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile, followed by speculative hydrolysis to free arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Full cyanosulfidic nitrile-first route\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete surface route that uses the experimentally observed but less efficient direct guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile before a speculative photoreductive hydrolysis rejoins the common late branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrylonitrile-entry aldehyde-first route\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry from accumulated acrylonitrile into the favored aldehyde-first branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrylonitrile-entry nitrile-first route\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry from accumulated acrylonitrile into the weaker nitrile-first branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Beta-aminopropionitrile-entry aldehyde-first route\",\n      \"description\": \"Late entry from accumulated beta-aminopropionitrile into the experimentally preferred aldehyde-first manifold.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Beta-aminopropionitrile-entry nitrile-first route\",\n      \"description\": \"Late entry from accumulated beta-aminopropionitrile into the experimentally weaker but still literature-anchored nitrile-first branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidino C3 aldehyde late-stage route\",\n      \"description\": \"Late-stage pathway from the guanidino C3 aldehyde adduct through homologation, Strecker conversion, and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Formal C5 guanidino aldehyde Strecker-hydrolysis route\",\n      \"description\": \"Endgame from the formal C5 guanidino aldehyde precursor to arginine via the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Arginine aminonitrile hydrolysis route\",\n      \"description\": \"Terminal conversion of preformed arginine alpha-aminonitrile to free arginine under mild aqueous conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Ornithine guanidinylation route\",\n      \"description\": \"Alternative late route in which surface-generated ornithine is directly guanidinylated by cyanamide in concentrated pools.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Arginine", "overall_strategy": "This network prioritizes surface cyanosulfidic chemistry because it is the only experimentally developed arginine-specific branch in the supplied literature: HCN plus acetylene gives acrylonitrile, which gives beta-aminopropionitrile, then a guanidino aldehyde manifold, then the arginine alpha-aminonitrile. The literature boundary is respected by marking the final hydrolysis to free arginine as speculative, since Patel et al. (2015) stop at the aminonitrile. In parallel, the network includes a late-stage ornithine-to-arginine guanidinylation route with cyanamide, also flagged as plausible but unverified for free ornithine. Ten pathways are listed as two full routes plus shorter valid entry routes from accumulated intermediates; disputed direct arginine claims from HCN polymer hydrolysates are not used as positive evidence, and hydrothermal routes are omitted because the supplied literature gives no arginine-specific kinetic pathway there.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "cyanamide", "common_name": "cyanamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h(H2,2,3)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H3N", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c4-2-1-3-5/h1-2,4H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "S", "iupac_name": "sulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": "InChI=1S/S", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H8N4", "iupac_name": "3-(carbamimidamido)propanenitrile", "common_name": "guanidinylated beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H9N3O", "iupac_name": "3-(carbamimidamido)propanal", "common_name": "guanidinylated beta-aminopropionaldehyde adduct", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H11N3O", "iupac_name": "N-(4-oxobutyl)guanidine", "common_name": "formal C5 guanidino aldehyde Strecker precursor", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C6H13N5", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-guanidinopentanenitrile", "common_name": "arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H12N2O2", "iupac_name": "2,5-diaminopentanoic acid", "common_name": "ornithine", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C6H14N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid", "common_name": "arginine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Cu(I)-catalyzed coupling of hydrogen cyanide with acetylene", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)", "phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I), phosphate", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-irradiated near-neutral surface stream or pool; locally cyanide-rich cyanosulfidic setting"}, "mechanism": "Copper-mediated C-C bond formation between HCN-derived cyanide and acetylene generates the activated nitrile alkene acrylonitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly use Cu(I)-catalyzed coupling of HCN with acetylene to make acrylonitrile 33 as the entry point to the arginine branch. A surficial UV-fed setting is preferred because the same chemistry is framed as local surface flow chemistry rather than hydrothermal vent chemistry; copper availability is a limitation, so this is best treated as a niche but experimentally grounded route (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly basic aqueous pool or stream; ammonia-rich"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia performs conjugate addition to the activated alkene of acrylonitrile, giving beta-aminopropionitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) report conjugate addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile to give beta-aminopropionitrile 34, the common branch point toward arginine-related chemistry. This is a straightforward Michael-type addition and is one of the better-supported steps in the branch."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Aldehyde-first formation of the guanidino C3 aldehyde adduct", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)", "phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I), phosphate", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation with HS-/H2S as stoichiometric photoreductant; sequential photoreductive nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion followed by cyanamide capture"}, "mechanism": "Net two-step sequence: photoreductive hydrolysis of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde, then reaction of the aldehyde-bearing amine with cyanamide to form the guanidino aldehyde adduct.", "reasoning": "This reaction is written as a balanced net summary of the experimentally supported aldehyde-first sequence in Patel et al. (2015): 34->36 by H2S/HS--driven photoreduction, then efficient capture of 36 by cyanamide to give 37. Patel et al. report 34->36 at 45% and the combined 34->37 sequence at 77% over two steps. This is the literature-preferred ordering because the aldehyde reacts with cyanamide efficiently, whereas direct guanidinylation of 34 is described as relatively inefficient (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Direct guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile with cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral aqueous pool; cyanamide present with ammonia-derived beta-aminopropionitrile"}, "mechanism": "The primary amine of beta-aminopropionitrile adds to cyanamide and rearranges/tautomerizes to the guanidino nitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) directly convert 34 to the guanidinylated nitrile 35 with cyanamide, but explicitly note that this guanidinylation is relatively inefficient and leaves residual starting material and cyanamide. It is included as an experimentally observed but weaker alternative branch."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Photoreductive hydrolysis of guanidino nitrile to the guanidino C3 aldehyde adduct", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)", "phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I), phosphate", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation in sulfide-rich surface water; speculative nitrile-first continuation"}, "mechanism": "Net reductive hydrolysis of the terminal nitrile to the corresponding aldehyde under HS-/H2S photoredox conditions, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Speculative. No experiment in the supplied literature directly reports 35->37, but the chemistry is a close analogue of Patel's demonstrated photoreductive nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion 34->36. The balanced net written here captures the same nitrile-to-aldehyde logic after prior guanidinylation. This branch is therefore chemically plausible but untested, and may compete poorly with side reactions or decomposition (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Net one-carbon homologation to the formal C5 guanidino aldehyde precursor", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)", "phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I), phosphate", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-irradiated sulfide-rich pool; formal net representation of the late homologation manifold"}, "mechanism": "HCN adds one carbon to the guanidino aldehyde manifold, and sulfide-driven photoredox chemistry effects the reductive homologation needed to reach a C5 guanidino aldehyde-equivalent Strecker precursor.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) place 37 on a late reductive homologation manifold proceeding through cyanohydrin/thioamide intermediates toward the arginine aminonitrile branch. The free aldehyde N-(4-oxobutyl)guanidine is not isolated there; in this network it is used as a formal node that summarizes the C4->C5 homologation state feeding the final Strecker step. This is therefore a literature-guided net step rather than a directly isolated one, but it follows the reported homologation logic and matches the independent use of the same aldehyde concept as the putative Strecker precursor in computational work (Patel et al., 2015; Cobb et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Strecker conversion to arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN/NH3-rich pool; near-neutral to mildly basic aqueous conditions"}, "mechanism": "The C5 guanidino aldehyde undergoes Strecker chemistry with ammonia and cyanide to give the corresponding alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) tabulate the late 45->46 conversion to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile at 90%. Because their exact isolated precursor is a late homologation intermediate rather than the free aldehyde formalized here, this node is represented as the corresponding Strecker event from the C5 guanidino aldehyde precursor. The chemistry is standard Strecker logic and is the strongest experimentally supported route currently available to an arginine-specific direct precursor (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile to arginine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Prolonged mild hydrolysis in surface pools or geothermal cycling; strong acid/base avoided"}, "mechanism": "The alpha-aminonitrile hydrates and hydrolyzes to the amino acid carboxylate level, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Speculative and explicitly the key unverified endgame. The supplied literature does not report hydrolysis of the arginine alpha-aminonitrile to free arginine under defended prebiotic conditions; Patel et al. (2015) stop at the aminonitrile, and the review emphasizes that no complete prebiotic synthesis of free arginine has been demonstrated. Mild hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles is chemically reasonable, but harsh acid/base treatment risks guanidino-group degradation to citrulline or ornithine, so this step is included as a necessary but presently unsupported closure (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Guanidinylation of ornithine with cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated evaporitic or geothermal pools; cyanamide-rich, slow aqueous guanidinylation expected"}, "mechanism": "The terminal amino group of ornithine adds to cyanamide and rearranges/tautomerizes to the guanidino side chain of arginine.", "reasoning": "Speculative but chemically attractive if ornithine is available. Ornithine itself is reported from spark-discharge and hot HCN/NH4CN chemistries, whereas arginine is not (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011; Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). Longo et al. (2020) showed aqueous ornithine-side-chain guanidination in a synthetic protein and argued that cyanamide could serve prebiotically; cyanamide availability is also supported by NH4CN photochemistry (Lohrmann, 1972). Patel et al. (2015) further show that cyanamide can guanidinylate aliphatic amines, albeit inefficiently. Free-ornithine delta-selective guanidinylation by cyanamide in water has not been directly demonstrated, so this route remains plausible but unverified (Longo et al., 2020; Patel et al., 2015; Thoma, 2023)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Full cyanosulfidic aldehyde-first route", "description": "Complete surface route from acetylene, HCN, NH3, cyanamide, H2S, and water through the experimentally favored aldehyde-first sequence to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile, followed by speculative hydrolysis to free arginine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Full cyanosulfidic nitrile-first route", "description": "Complete surface route that uses the experimentally observed but