.researchgate.net/publication/9309353_The_Hydrolysis_of_Arginine)\n32. [Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein ...](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=167961801406af19852f76f62e796fa905c99643&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\n33. [Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein amino acids - ADS](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JMolE..17..273W)\n34. [Consensus temporal order of amino acids and evolution of the triplet code](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12166018_Consensus_temporal_order_of_amino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n35. [Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n\nKey Citations:\n  1. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism - Nature Chemistry (https://www.nature.com/articles/nchem.2202)\n  2. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4568310/)\n  3. Chemical evolution (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01797455)\n  4. Chemical evolution: XXI. The Amino Acids Released on Hydrolysis of HCN Oligomers - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1974JMolE...3..225F/abstract)\n  5. Possible role of hydrogen cyanide in chemical evolution: Investigation of the proposed direct synthesis of peptides from hydrogen cyanide - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0022283673900429)\n  6. (PDF) Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H 2 S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.academia.edu/91151237/Primordial_synthesis_of_amines_and_amino_acids_in_a_1958_Miller_H_2_S_rich_spark_discharge_experiment)\n  7. (PDF) The Miller Volcanic Spark Discharge Experiment (https://www.academia.edu/121732208/The_Miller_Volcanic_Spark_Discharge_Experiment)\n  8. Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H2S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1019191108)\n  9. Current status of the prebiotic synthesis of small molecules - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11542054/)\n  10. Primordial emergence of a nucleic acid-binding protein via phase separation and statistical ornithine-to-arginine conversion (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2001989117)\n  11. Primordial emergence of a nucleic acid-binding protein via phase separation and statistical ornithine-to-arginine conversion (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/342323056_Primordial_emergence_of_a_nucleic_acid-binding_protein_via_phase_separation_and_statistical_ornithine-to-arginine_conversion)\n  12. (PDF) Primordial emergence of a nucleic acid binding protein via phase separation and statistical ornithine to arginine conversion (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/338687343_Primordial_emergence_of_a_nucleic_acid_binding_protein_via_phase_separation_and_statistical_ornithine_to_arginine_conversion)\n  13. Chemical studies into the aetiology of proteinogenic amino acids Benjamin Thoma (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10185022/2/B_Thoma_Thesis_Corrected.pdf)\n  14. Selective Synthesis of Lysine Peptides and the Prebiotically Plausible Synthesis of Catalytically Active Diaminopropionic Acid Peptide Nitriles in Water (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/jacs.2c12497)\n  15. The Urea Cycle Step by Step (https://www.news-medical.net/health/The-Urea-Cycle-Step-by-Step.aspx)\n  16. Sources and Fates of Carbamyl Phosphate: A Labile Energy-Rich Molecule with Multiple Facets (https://www.mdpi.com/2079-7737/7/2/34)\n  17. Prebiotic Synthesis of Aspartate Using Life\u2019s Metabolism as a Guide (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10221237/)\n  18. Quantifying Reductive Amination in Nonenzymatic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9828492/)\n  19. A plausible metal-free ancestral analogue of the Krebs cycle composed entirely of \u03b1-ketoacids (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-020-00560-7)\n  20. Formation of urea and guanidine by irradiation of ammonium cyanide (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01660246)\n  21. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a (https://europepmc.org/api/getPdf?pmcid=PMC4568310)\n  22. Cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanosulfidic_prebiotic_synthesis)\n  23. Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.07725)\n  24. Guanidine synthesis by guanylation (https://www.organic-chemistry.org/synthesis/C1N/guanidines.shtm)\n  25. Extraterrestrial amino acids and L\u2010enantiomeric excesses in the \\<fc\\>CM\\</fc\\>2 carbonaceous chondrites Aguas Zarcas and Murchison - Glavin - 2021 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/maps.13451)\n  26. The stereochemistry of amino acids in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0301926800001236)\n  27. Purines and triazines in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0016703775901015)\n  28. Extraterrestrial nucleobases in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012821X08001866?via=ihub%3D)\n  29. (PDF) The Hydrolysis of Arginine (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/9309353_The_Hydrolysis_of_Arginine)\n  30. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein ... (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=167961801406af19852f76f62e796fa905c99643&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\n  31. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein amino acids - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JMolE..17..273W)\n  32. Consensus temporal order of amino acids and evolution of the triplet code (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12166018_Consensus_temporal_order_of_amino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.49\n- Feedback: The network topology is acceptable: 13 molecule nodes and 11 reaction nodes form a connected graph with consistent IDs. Literature alignment is better than the chemistry implementation: the strategy appropriately centers Patel-style cyanosulfidic chemistry, acknowledges that only the arginine alpha-aminonitrile is experimentally supported, treats ornithine guanidinylation as speculative, and does not rely on disputed direct-arginine claims. The limiting problem is validity and stoichiometry. RDKit finds 6 of 11 reactions unbalanced: rxn_001 likely needs H2 as coproduct; rxn_004 and rxn_007 cannot be represented as simple H2S-only reductions and likely omit H2O, NH3 and sulfur byproducts; rxn_008 is missing at least one carbon equivalent and compresses the 37 -> 45 -> 46 sequence into an unsupported one-step net; rxn_009 should include 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as product; rxn_010 is grossly under-specified and not a credible single-step ornithine synthesis. rxn_011 is balanced but still unverified for free ornithine with cyanamide, and delta-selectivity is unresolved. Surface conditions are the best fit for the Patel-like chemistry, but the final Biochemical steps are not mapped onto defended prebiotic environments. Best branch is P2, but even that route only plausibly reaches the arginine alpha-aminonitrile logic, not defended free arginine. Not approved as submitted.\n- Issues: ['Best endogenous de novo branch conceptually because it follows the literature-supported Patel aldehyde-first logic from acrylonitrile to intermediate 37 and then toward the arginine alpha-aminonitrile. The main problems are stoichiometric: rxn_001 is unbalanced and likely needs H2 as a coproduct, rxn_004 is not a valid H2S-only one-step conversion and likely omits H2O, NH3 and sulfur byproducts, rxn_008 collapses the documented multistep 37 -> 45 -> 46 chemistry into an unbalanced net that is short one carbon, and rxn_009 should include 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as product. Literature alignment is good, but free arginine is not actually defended as formed here, and the final Biochemical step is not placed in a specific prebiotic setting.', 'This is the strongest pathway in the network and the closest match to the Patel literature because it uses the experimentally favored aldehyde-first order. However, rxn_004, rxn_008 and rxn_009 are still unbalanced; rxn_008 in particular compresses several Patel steps and is short one carbon, while rxn_009 needs 2 H2O in and NH3 out. If cyanamide is intended to be environmental here, mol_004 should be declared as an explicit starting input or justified transfer. Surface chemistry is plausible up to the aminonitrile logic, but the final Biochemical hydrolysis is not a defended prebiotic environment.', 'The early steps to guanidinylated nitrile 35 are literature-supported, but Patel actually favors aldehyde-first capture because the 36 plus cyanamide reaction is efficient. This nitrile-first branch depends on rxn_007, an explicitly speculative and unbalanced 35-to-37 type reduction that likely omits H2O, NH3 and sulfur byproducts, then inherits the unbalanced compressed rxn_008 and the unbalanced hydrolysis rxn_009, which should include 2 H2O in and NH3 out. rxn_001 also needs H2 as coproduct if cyanamide is generated in-network.', 'Slightly cleaner than P3 only because it omits in-network cyanamide formation, but it remains substantially weaker than the aldehyde-first branch. rxn_007 is unbalanced and unsupported by the cited Patel chemistry, rxn_008 is an unbalanced one-step compression of a longer sequence and is short one carbon, and rxn_009 needs 2 H2O in and NH3 out. As written, this route is a speculative extrapolation rather than an experimentally grounded arginine pathway, and environmental cyanamide should be made explicit if that assumption is intended.', 'Strategically honest about the ornithine endgame being contested, but chemically weak as encoded. rxn_010 is grossly unbalanced and does not represent any single literature route from HCN plus NH3 to ornithine; ornithine reports come from HCN-polymer hydrolysates or spark residues, not direct one-step conversion. rxn_011 is balanced and chemically conceivable, but free ornithine guanidinylation by cyanamide in water remains unverified, alpha versus delta selectivity is unresolved, and the Biochemical environment label is too vague to count as a defended prebiotic setting. rxn_001 also needs H2 as coproduct.', 'This is the cleaner ornithine endgame because it avoids in-network cyanamide formation, but the core weakness remains. rxn_010 is still a severely unbalanced and under-specified stand-in for trace ornithine reports from very different chemistries, while rxn_011, although balanced, is not experimentally demonstrated for free ornithine under prebiotic aqueous conditions and has unresolved site selectivity. If cyanamide is environmental, that input should be explicit rather than implied.', 'As a module, this tracks the strongest Patel ordering from acrylonitrile through the