n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) use acrylonitrile 33 as the branch-point precursor and explicitly identify beta-aminopropionitrile 34 as the entry to proline/lysine/arginine-related chemistry. This is one of the most secure steps in the arginine-specific network and is straightforward in aqueous ammonia (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"HS-/UV reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated cyanosulfidic surface setting; H2S/HS- as stoichiometric photoreductant\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox partial reduction of the nitrile followed by hydrolytic release of ammonia yields the aminoaldehyde; sulfur is the formal oxidized sulfur sink.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report HS-/H2S-driven photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile 34 to beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36 in 45% yield. The exact sulfur bookkeeping is not given in the paper, so this node is written as a balanced net with elemental sulfur as the oxidized sulfur product. The surficial sulfide requirement is plausible only in local settings, as discussed by Ranjan et al. (2018), with later related photoredox substitutions by sulfite/ferrocyanide shown by Xu et al. (2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldehyde-first cyanamide capture\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanamide present together with the aminoaldehyde in a surface pool; near-neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanamide reacts with the aminoaldehyde to generate a formal guanidino-aldehyde adduct represented here as open-chain intermediate 37.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) note that residual cyanamide reacts very efficiently with beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36 to give adduct 37, and this ordering is the favored branch toward the arginine aminonitrile. Because the supplied review does not resolve the exact solution-state tautomer or cyclized form of 37, the graph uses an open-chain stoichiometric surrogate for the adduct while preserving the reported reaction logic (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanamide in aqueous solution; no added activating reagent\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Addition of the primary amine to cyanamide gives the guanidinylated nitrile 35.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly report conversion of beta-aminopropionitrile 34 into guanidinylated nitrile 35 with cyanamide, but they describe the step as relatively inefficient and mixture-forming. It is therefore included as a real but less favored alternative to the aldehyde-first branch (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative HS-/UV reduction of guanidino nitrile 35 to aldehyde-level intermediate 37\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfide-rich surface pool; written as a balanced net analogous to nitrile-to-aldehyde photoreduction\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal photoredox partial reduction/hydrolysis of the nitrile gives the same aldehyde-level guanidino intermediate reached by the aldehyde-first route.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. No provided paper isolates this exact one-step conversion from 35 to 37. It is included as a chemically reasonable extrapolation of the Patel et al. (2015) nitrile-to-aldehyde reduction logic, but the literature much more strongly supports the aldehyde-first sequence 34 -> 36 -> 37 than this nitrile-first reduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Telescoped reductive homologation from intermediate 37 to cyanohydrin 45\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), phosphate buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated cyanosulfidic surface chemistry; balanced net corresponds to mol_011 + 2 mol_001 + 2 mol_004 -> mol_012 + mol_002 + 2 mol_008\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential HCN addition, sulfide-driven photoreduction, and re-homologation are collapsed into a single balanced net that reaches the C6 cyanohydrin 45.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) describe continued reductive homologation from 37 through thioamide and cyanohy