 "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I), phosphate buffer", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-irradiated cyanosulfidic surface setting; H2S/HS- as stoichiometric photoreductant"}, "mechanism": "Photoredox partial reduction of the nitrile followed by hydrolytic release of ammonia yields the aminoaldehyde; sulfur is the formal oxidized sulfur sink.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) report HS-/H2S-driven photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile 34 to beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36 in 45% yield. The exact sulfur bookkeeping is not given in the paper, so this node is written as a balanced net with elemental sulfur as the oxidized sulfur product. The surficial sulfide requirement is plausible only in local settings, as discussed by Ranjan et al. (2018), with later related photoredox substitutions by sulfite/ferrocyanide shown by Xu et al. (2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Aldehyde-first cyanamide capture", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanamide present together with the aminoaldehyde in a surface pool; near-neutral pH"}, "mechanism": "Cyanamide reacts with the aminoaldehyde to generate a formal guanidino-aldehyde adduct represented here as open-chain intermediate 37.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) note that residual cyanamide reacts very efficiently with beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36 to give adduct 37, and this ordering is the favored branch toward the arginine aminonitrile. Because the supplied review does not resolve the exact solution-state tautomer or cyclized form of 37, the graph uses an open-chain stoichiometric surrogate for the adduct while preserving the reported reaction logic (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Direct guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": 