queous": true, "other": "prebiotic pool or geothermal cycling; balanced net corresponds to mol_013 + 2 mol_005 -> mol_015 + mol_002"}, "mechanism": "Stepwise nitrile hydrolysis gives the carboxylic acid and liberates ammonia.", "reasoning": "Speculative endgame. Patel et al. (2015) stop at the arginine alpha-aminonitrile and do not report free arginine. Aminonitrile hydrolysis to amino acids is general chemistry, but the review emphasizes that arginine itself can further degrade under harsher hydrolytic conditions, so only mild aqueous hydrolysis under pool or geothermal cycling is defensible. This step is included because a target-level network requires it, not because it is experimentally validated in the supplied arginine literature."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Late guanidination of ornithine with cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "concentrated prebiotic pool or mineral interface; mildly basic conditions likely most favorable; alpha/delta selectivity unresolved"}, "mechanism": "Amidination/guanidination of the side-chain amine of ornithine by cyanamide furnishes the guanidino side chain of arginine.", "reasoning": "Speculative but chemically tractable late route. Longo et al. (2020) showed aqueous guanidination of ornithine side chains in a synthetic protein and argued that cyanamide could serve as a primordial guanidinating reagent; Thoma (2023) reports selective ornithine-to-arginine peptide chemistry, but no peer-reviewed demonstration exists for free ornithine + cyanamide in water. Ornithine itself is at least a plausible prebiotic substrate because it was detected in archived Miller spark samples and older cyanide-based experiments, whereas arginine was not (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011; Longo et al., 2020)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Acr